INSERT INTO "prompt_templates" ("name","description","variables","content","slug","id","inserted_at","updated_at") VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8) ["In-Progress Dispatch Prompt", "Sent when a task is in_progress with an approved plan. Instructs the agent to execute the current stage and push evidence.", ["task_id", "stage_id", "task_title", "stage_info", "stage_description", "migration_slot_section", "git_workflow_block", "provider_specific_guidance", "federation_preamble", "feedback_guidance"], "**Your first tool call must be `suite_task_ack(task_id=\"{{task_id}}\", stage_id=\"{{stage_id}}\", subagent_id=\"<the id returned when you spawned the SuiteExecutor / SuiteReviewer via the Agent tool>\")`.**\n\nSpawn the subagent FIRST, then pass the id of the subagent you actually spawned as `subagent_id`. The server rejects only a missing, empty, whitespace-only, non-string or over-128-byte subagent_id; it does not verify that the id names a real spawn, so spawn-before-ack ordering is your discipline, not a server guarantee. Without a successful ack the router will fire a no-ack alert in 5 minutes.\n\n**If `suite_task_ack` is missing from your MCP tool cache** (a known cross-version issue where a session's catalog predates a new tool), call `suite_alarm_silence(task_id=\"{{task_id}}\", stage_id=\"{{stage_id}}\", until_seconds=1800, reason=\"task_ack absent from stale MCP catalog; subagent live\")` as the operator escape hatch. Do NOT use `report_blocker(needs_human: false)` — that anti-pattern sets a task-level lease to blocked and leaks across stage boundaries.\n\nPlan approved — execute the current stage.\n\nTask: {{task_title}}\n{{stage_info}}\n{{stage_description}}\n{{provider_specific_guidance}}\n\nPush evidence using validation_pass or stage_complete as you finish each step. Post commentary to the execution space so reviewers can follow along. Use report_blocker if you are stuck.\n\n## Design fidelity (when an Attached design canvases block is present)\n\nIf this dispatch carries an Attached design canvases block and this stage changes rendered UI:\n1. Screenshot every canvas FIRST, with that block's render recipe, and post the screenshots to the execution space before your first commit.\n2. Build to the stage description's inventory: every element, every state and every verbatim string it lists.\n3. At stage end, post a message to the execution space headed `Design deviations — stage <n>` that lists, for each place the implementation does not match, `canvas <id> node <node id> | what differs | why`. If there is none, post `Design deviations — stage <n>: none`.\n4. Put the same list in the evidence of your last `validation_pass` for this stage, under `design_deviations` (`stage_complete` takes no evidence argument).\n\nAn undeclared difference fails the fidelity case at task review. Without that block, or on a stage that changes no rendered UI, this section does not apply.\n{{migration_slot_section}}\n{{git_workflow_block}}\nThe Stage Description block above is your specification — it is the planner's own authored text for this stage, and it is the authority over any summary of it. The full task hierarchy (project, epic, task body, whole plan) is NOT in this message; fetch it with suite_task_get / suite_plan_get if you need it.\nStart from that stage description and any attached/bundled skill content before re-fetching broad task/project context.\nAvoid redundant task/plan lookup churn on the first turn — but if the Stage Description block is absent, empty, or looks cut off, call suite_plan_get and read the stage description there before doing anything else.\n\n{{federation_preamble}}{{feedback_guidance}}\n", "dispatch.in_progress", "01a18f2c-95db-7000-886f-e82c1f71e902", ~U[2026-10-30 23:37:06.267842Z], ~U[2026-10-30 23:37:06.267842Z]]

