INSERT INTO "prompt_templates" ("name","description","variables","content","slug","id","inserted_at","updated_at") VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8) ["Deploying Dispatch Prompt", "Sent when a task is in deploying status with a running deploy stage. Instructs the agent to execute the deploy based on the resolved strategy.", ["task_id", "stage_id", "task_title", "stage_info", "repo_url", "default_branch", "task_slug", "deploy_strategy_instructions", "deploy_strategy_type", "deploy_strategy_config", "federation_preamble", "feedback_guidance"], "**Your first tool call must be `suite_task_ack(task_id=\"{{task_id}}\", stage_id=\"{{stage_id}}\", subagent_id=\"<the id returned when you spawned the SuiteExecutor / SuiteReviewer via the Agent tool>\")`.**\n\nSpawn the subagent FIRST, then pass the id of the subagent you actually spawned as `subagent_id`. The server rejects only a missing, empty, whitespace-only, non-string or over-128-byte subagent_id; it does not verify that the id names a real spawn, so spawn-before-ack ordering is your discipline, not a server guarantee. Without a successful ack the router will fire a no-ack alert in 5 minutes.\n\n**If `suite_task_ack` is missing from your MCP tool cache** (a known cross-version issue where a session's catalog predates a new tool), call `suite_alarm_silence(task_id=\"{{task_id}}\", stage_id=\"{{stage_id}}\", until_seconds=1800, reason=\"task_ack absent from stale MCP catalog; subagent live\")` as the operator escape hatch. Do NOT use `report_blocker(needs_human: false)` — that anti-pattern sets a task-level lease to blocked and leaks across stage boundaries.\n\nTask is deploying — execute the deploy stage based on the resolved strategy.\n\nTask: {{task_title}}\n{{stage_info}}Deploy strategy: **{{deploy_strategy_type}}**\nStrategy config: {{deploy_strategy_config}}\n\n### Strategy-specific instructions\n\n{{deploy_strategy_instructions}}\n\n### Merge mechanics on Startup-Suite/core (overrides any \"auto-merge is enabled\" line above)\n- `auto_merge: true` in the strategy config is a declaration, not an action. Nothing arms GitHub auto-merge except an explicit `gh pr merge <n> --auto`. Arming is safe on this repository ONLY while `main`'s required status checks include BOTH `Test` and `first-run-green` (added 2026-09-11; `first-run-green` is what makes a failed-then-retried `Test` run block the merge, ticket 01a08d81). Verify before arming, every time: `gh api repos/Startup-Suite/core/branches/main/protection/required_status_checks --jq .contexts` must print both. If it does, arm with `gh pr merge <n> --auto --squash --delete-branch` and still watch CI to conclusion. If `first-run-green` is missing from the contexts, do NOT arm: wait for CI in the foreground, read the annotations for the retry sentinel, and merge by hand.\n- Wait for CI in the foreground: `gh run watch <run-id> --exit-status` (or `gh pr checks <n> --watch`). Never end your turn \"waiting on CI\"; an ended turn is a dead deployer.\n- Before merging (or before reporting an armed merge as clean), read the run's ANNOTATIONS (not a whole-log grep) for `initial mix test failed`. If it fired, name the tests the retry greened. There is no known-flake list as of task 01a13aac (the four names it used to carry had 0 retry-green occurrences in 320 CI runs): a test the retry greened is a red run. Name it in the PR body, file it as a task, and it goes back to the ONE rule below.\n- Verify the merge as a field, not an exit code: `gh pr view <n> --json mergedAt,mergeCommit --jq '.mergedAt'` must print a timestamp. `mergeStateStatus: BLOCKED` while checks are pending is normal; it matters only if it survives a `success` conclusion. Confirm the remote branch is gone afterwards; the per-repository steps above say how.\n- The squash commit does not carry the PR body; anything that must survive in `git log` goes in a commit message.\n\n### Deploy boundaries\n- If CI fails on the deploy branch, there is ONE rule. Read the failing job log first. If the failure is inside this task's own diff, or it is a flaky test with a documented retry (an existing retry step, a known-flake note in org memory, or an identical pass on re-run), fix forward on the same task branch (the one named below): make the smallest change that turns it green, push, and wait for CI to go green, then continue the merge. Do not touch code outside the task's diff. Only if the failure needs a plan change (the fix would add scope, change a design decision, or touch files no stage in the plan owns) call `task_update` with status `in_progress` and a comment naming the failing job and why a plan change is needed; that is what returns the task to the " <> ..., "dispatch.deploying", "01a18f2c-95e1-7000-9771-615c8b34de02", ~U[2026-10-30 23:37:06.273635Z], ~U[2026-10-30 23:37:06.273635Z]]

